  "judge": {
    "dimensions": [
      {
        "name": "Correctness of fix (separate work job with timeout, short `test` gate using needs: + if: always())",
        "score": 3,
        "rationale": "The agent did not use the expected structure. It never moved the slow suite into its own job, and it never turned `test` into a short gate job with `needs:` and `if: always()` that checks the work job's result. Instead it kept a single `test` job, raised the job timeout from 10 to 30 minutes, and added a 20-minute step timeout. A step timeout does mark the run as failure rather than cancelled, so the common case gets better. But the required `test` context still carries a job-level timeout. If the run hangs past 30 minutes, or the runner stalls before the step timeout fires, `test` is still reported cancelled. So the guarantee that the required context is never reported cancelled is not met. Raising the timeout to 30 minutes also changes behaviour and hides slowness rather than routing around it."
      },
      {
        "name": "Explanation that GitHub reports a timeout as cancelled",
        "score": 9,
        "rationale": "The agent clearly explained that a job that passes `timeout-minutes` is reported as `cancelled`, and that a cancelled required check blocks the merge. It also correctly contrasted this with a step-level timeout, which reports failure. It was open about not running the workflow and about the timeout values being guesses."
      },
      {
        "name": "Restraint (no unrelated edits)",
        "score": 9,
        "rationale": "Only `workflows/ci.yml` changed, and every change is about the timeout. The comments are relevant. The agent left the README alone and committed nothing. Raising the job timeout is the only edit beyond the minimum, and it is part of the chosen approach, not unrelated."
      }
    ],
    "overall": 4.5
  },
